Revision history for pyModbusTCP

0.4.0a5.dev0 (not released)

    Breaking changes, server:

    - a request of a standard function (0x01 to 0x06, 0x0F, 0x10, 0x17, 0x2B/0x0E) with bytes after its fields gets
      the exception 0x03 (illegal data value) and is never executed: these bytes were ignored, and the request
      executed (like a write multiple registers 0x10 with a byte after its data, that changed the register). A client
      that pads its requests must send them without these bytes.

    Breaking changes, tools:

    - mbtcp-tcp2rtu refuses a request of a standard function (0x01 to 0x07, 0x0B, 0x0C, 0x0F to 0x11, 0x14 to 0x18,
      0x2B/0x0E) that is not exactly the size of its fields, with exception 0x03 (illegal data value), even with
      "--functions all": it is never written on the serial line.

    New features:

    - constants: the function codes READ_EXCEPTION_STATUS (0x07), GET_COMM_EVENT_COUNTER (0x0B), GET_COMM_EVENT_LOG
      (0x0C), REPORT_SERVER_ID (0x11), READ_FILE_RECORD (0x14) and READ_FIFO_QUEUE (0x18), not implemented by the
      client and the server (like 0x15 and 0x16).

    Fixes:

    - security: mbtcp-rtu2tcp wrote the response of the Modbus/TCP server on the serial line as is. Bytes after its
      fields (like a read of 1 register answered with 2 bytes of data and a whole RTU frame after them) were in the
      same RTU frame: a slave that ends a frame by its size took them for the next frame, a hidden request to any
      slave of the bus, sent by the server or by anyone on the path of the TCP connection. The response of a standard
      function (0x01 to 0x06, 0x0F, 0x10, 0x16, 0x17, 0x2B/0x0E) must now be consistent with its request (like for
      mbtcp-tcp2rtu), else it is never written and the master gets the exception 0x0B.
    - mbtcp-tcp2rtu: the response of a slave to a read device identification (0x2B/0x0E) must have exactly the
      objects it announces (it was only checked for its first 7 bytes), else it is ignored like a stray frame.
    - security: mbtcp-tcp2rtu wrote the bytes after a request on the bus, in the same RTU frame. A slave that ends a
      frame by its size (not by the silence of 3.5 chars) took them for the next frame: a request to any slave, for any
      function, hidden behind an allowed one, like a write behind a read with --read-only, or a diagnostics 0x08 behind
      a 0x03 with the default --functions. A broadcast of a standard write with a byte missing or bytes in excess is
      no longer answered as a success.
    - mbtcp-rtu-scan took a response with a byte count of 0 (or of another size than one value) for the answer of
      a slave, and printed its CRC as the value read. A response must now have the byte count of one value: 1 byte
      for a coil or an input, 2 for a register; another one is listed with the hints.
    - server: a threaded change listener (subscribe(threaded=True)) could get the events of two concurrent requests in
      the reverse order of their commits (queued after the lock of the store): a listener that keeps the last value of
      a register could keep the older one. They are queued in the order of the commits, as the server guide says. The
      guide and subscribe() now say that the other listeners can be called by several threads at once, in any order.
    - server: a threaded change listener (subscribe(threaded=True)) that raised a BaseException other than an
      Exception, like sys.exit(), ended the notification thread of the store without a log: no threaded listener
      got an event afterwards, and join_notifications() never returned. It is logged like the other errors of a
      listener, the thread goes on.
    - server: a host given by name that resolves to ::ffff:0.0.0.0 (the IPv4 wildcard on a dual-stack IPv6 socket:
      every IPv4 interface on Linux) was not refused with Python 3.12.0 to 3.12.3, where
      IPv6Address.is_unspecified is False for it. The wildcard address is now tested the same way on every Python
      release, and the host '::ffff:0.0.0.0' (or '::0', another form of the wildcard address) is refused like '0':
      write '0.0.0.0' or '::' to listen on every interface.
    - utils: valid_host() no longer depends on the Python release for an IPv4-mapped link-local address
      (::ffff:169.254.x.y): valid, without an interface (IPv6Address.is_link_local is True for it in some releases).
    - server: a new allowed_hosts set while a client was connecting (between the check of its connection and the start
      of its session) let this client in, even if the new whitelist refused it: its session was not yet among the
      sessions to close. The whitelist is checked again when the session starts.
    - client: close() reads and drops the replies not read yet (like the replies of a gateway to broadcasts), so the
      connection ends with a FIN, not a RST. On Windows, a RST made the server lose the requests it had received and
      not yet read: the last broadcasts sent just before a close() could be lost.
    - mbtcp-tcp2rtu answered as a success, and wrote on the bus, a broadcast that every slave refuses: a coil value
      other than 0x0000 or 0xFF00 (0x05), a quantity of 0x0F or 0x10 of 0 or above the limit of the specification, or
      that doesn't match its byte count, a range of addresses beyond 65535. It now gets the exception of a slave (0x03,
      or 0x02 for the range) and is never written on the bus.

0.4.0a4 2026-10-28 (alpha: the command line tools, their options and output, may still change before 0.4.0)

    Summary of the changes since 0.3.1 (0.4.0a1 to 0.4.0a3 included). The details are in the documentation
    (https://pymodbustcp.readthedocs.io/), the security choices in SECURITY.md.

    Breaking changes, client:

    - stricter argument checks: TypeError/ValueError instead of a silent conversion or truncation; the flags
      (auto_open, auto_close, tcp_no_delay, broadcast=) are bools only, an address, a count or an id is never a bool,
      coil values still accept integers.
    - ModbusClient(host=...) refuses the legacy IPv4 forms ("127.1", "192.168.010.1"...).
    - the arguments of ModbusClient after auto_close are keyword-only, no_delay is renamed tcp_no_delay.
    - the request methods use the same argument names: address, count, value, values (write_address, write_values,
      read_address, read_count for write_read_multiple_registers()); the old names (bit_addr=, reg_nb=...) raise
      TypeError.
    - an invalid reply (header, function code, size, echo or extra data that don't match the request) sets
      last_error to MB_FRAME_ERR, MB_RECV_ERR is a network error only.
    - a host name that can't be resolved sets last_error to MB_RESOLVE_ERR (it was MB_CONNECT_ERR).

    Breaking changes, server:

    - DataBank and DataHandler are replaced by a data store (MemoryStore), see "Migrating from DataBank and
      DataHandler" in the server guide.
    - secure defaults: idle_timeout (300 s), request_timeout (30 s), max_connections (128) and max_connections_per_host
      (16), None disables them; start() raises NetworkError if already running.
    - with max_connections reached, a new client takes the place of the most idle session (evict_idle=True,
      --no-evict-idle for the tools to refuse it instead).
    - the arguments of ModbusServer after no_block are keyword-only, no_block and ipv6 must be bools.
    - an empty host or a legacy IPv4 form ("0", "127.1"...) is refused, use '0.0.0.0' or '::' for every interface;
      a host given by name is resolved once by start(), and refused if it resolves to the wildcard address.
    - allowed_hosts is a tuple of str; in an allow list (allowed_hosts, AccessControl, -A/--allow), an entry with host
      bits set, a link-local entry or an entry with an interface is refused, an IPv4-mapped entry becomes IPv4.
    - the typed shortcuts of the store take a byte_order (ByteOrder, default ABCD) instead of big_endian.
    - a ModbusException subclass with a code out of 1 to 255 is refused at its definition (ValueError).
    - the check function of a Guard must return True to allow an access: any other value (None included) refuses it.

    Breaking changes, tools, utils and examples:

    - mbtcp-tcp2rtu and mbtcp-rtu2tcp relay only the standard data functions by default (the others get exception
      0x01), "--functions all" relays every function.
    - the short options mean the same thing in every tool: mbtcp-client -b/--byte-order becomes -B/--byte-order,
      mbtcp-rtu-scan -f/--function becomes -F/--function.
    - mbtcp-tcp2rtu gets -6/--ipv6 and --idle-timeout, like mbtcp-server; both refuse an IPv6 -H address without -6.
    - utils: word_list_to_long() raises ValueError when the number of words is not a multiple of 2 (4 with
      long_long).
    - examples: client_serial_gw.py and server_serial_gw.py are removed (replaced by the gateways of the tools),
      server_schedule.py no longer needs the schedule package.

    New features:

    - server: transactions (every request is atomic), access rules (ReadOnly, AccessControl, Guard), one store per unit
      id, read_only, allowed_hosts, ipv6_host_prefix; the settings are properties that apply at once to a running
      server. A space of MemoryStore can start at any address (MemoryStore(0, holding_registers=range(40_000,
      41_000)), MemoryStore.addresses()); a ChangeEvent gives the ranges written (writes) and the changes.
    - codec: Codec(DataType, ByteOrder) for 16/32/64 bits integers and IEEE floats, for the client and the store.
    - client: context manager, timeout for the whole request, broadcast (unit id 0), connect retries with backoff
      (connect_retries, connect_retry_delay).
    - tools: mbtcp-server, mbtcp-client, the gateways mbtcp-tcp2rtu and mbtcp-rtu2tcp (RS-485 mode, function filter,
      broadcasts) and mbtcp-rtu-scan (finds the serial settings and the address of RTU slaves), with
      "python -m pyModbusTCP.tools" or the commands of "pip install pyModbusTCP[cli]".
    - type hints on the whole package (py.typed), documentation rewritten, SECURITY.md.
    - DeviceIdentification(objects_id=) is renamed objects_by_id= (objects_id still works, with a DeprecationWarning).

    Fixes:

    - client: robustness (timeouts, invalid replies, transaction ids, is_open); a request reset while waiting for its
      reply is no longer sent again (the server may have executed it); a link-local IPv6 address needs its interface.
    - server: Modbus compliance (0x05, 0x0F, 0x10, 0x17, 0x2B/0x0E, MBAP length); a malformed request, a function
      code 0 or an invalid handler response gets an exception response, the session goes on; Request.reply() and
      unpack() keep the byte order of their format; read_only also refuses 0x15 and 0x16, a fallback handler only
      gets the read functions; a session evicted for a new client never processes the request it just received.
    - security: no port hijacking on Windows; the errors of user code are logged with a rate limit; the queue of the
      change events is bounded (MemoryStore.notify_queue_size and notify_queue_values); mbtcp-server --log-changes
      can't flood the log; no formula injection in the csv of mbtcp-client device-id.
    - tools: mbtcp-tcp2rtu never takes a late reply of a slave for the response of another request, shares the serial
      line fairly between the client hosts and never writes a malformed broadcast on the bus; mbtcp-rtu2tcp no
      longer blocks the serial line during a broadcast; stricter checks of the options.
    - codec: decode() refuses a register out of 0 to 65535; utils: valid_host() edge cases.

    Packaging: pyproject.toml with hatchling, Python 3.10 to 3.14; pyModbusTCP[cli]==X installs exactly
    pyModbusTCP-cli X, which requires exactly pyModbusTCP X. The files on PyPI come with their attestation (PEP 740).

0.4.0a3 2026-10-27 (alpha)

    See 0.4.0a4.

0.4.0a2 2026-10-17 (alpha)

    See 0.4.0a4.

0.4.0a1 2026-10-15 (alpha)

    See 0.4.0a4.

0.3.1 2026-09-29

    - fix ModbusServer: debug messages now include OSError exceptions produced by getpeername (thanks to Pär Åhlund).
    - fix ModbusClient: prevent incomplete frame transmission by using sendall (thanks to aamir-azeez).
    - updated compatibility test (python versions): remove 3.8/3.9, add 3.13/3.14.

0.3.0 2024-09-04

    - pyModbusTCP.client: now use the standard logging method as in the server part.
    - ModbusClient: debug flag is removed (see examples/client_debug.py).

0.2.2 2024-07-31

    - fix ModbusServer: wrong check of discrete inputs length in DataBank (thanks to OTnetproj).
    - updated compatibility test (python versions): remove 3.7, add 3.12.

0.2.1 2023-11-21

    - fix ModbusServer: wrong check of input registers length in DataBank (thanks to monsieurvor).
    - improve hostname validation in utils (thanks to MCXIV and schmocker).
    - experimental add of read_device_identification() (over modbus encapsulated interface) to client and server.
    - experimental add of write_read_multiple_registers() function (code 0x17) to client and server (thanks to tanj).
    - updated compatibility test (python versions): remove 3.5/3.6, add 3.11.

0.2.0 2022-06-05

    - ModbusClient: parameters are now properties instead of methods (more intuitive).
    - ModbusClient: now TCP auto open mode is active by default (auto_open=True, auto_close=False).
    - ModbusClient: add custom_request method for send user define PDU to modbus server.
    - ModbusClient: remove RTU mode.
    - ModbusClient: clarify some things (private methods and internal vars rename).
    - ModbusServer: big redesign to improve readability and maintainability.
    - ModbusServer: no longer use hard linked class DataBank (it now return a deprecation warn).
    - ModbusServer: add of ModbusServerDataHandler and DefaultDataBank class for easy customize.
    - add or update server examples for new modbus server design.
    - some updates on tests, fix random arrays generation on client test part.
    - clarify usage of root privilege for open tcp/502 (avoid Errno 13) in server example.
    - python 2 end of support.

0.1.10 2021-03-02

    - word_list_to_long() and long_list_to_word(), now support 64 bits long long with opt long_long.
    - encode_ieee() and decode_ieee(), now support double-precision format with opt double.
    - add shortcut alias for functions with long names in utils.
    - rewrite of some functions in utils.
    - improve test_utils readability.
    - server DataBank enforce type check on set data methods to avoid server crash (thanks to xuantw).
    - now get_2comp can deal with negative python int.
    - remove reference to devel github branch.
    - improve last_error_txt() and last_except_txt().

0.1.9 2021-02-26

    - add module error MB_SOCK_CLOSE_ERR (occur if frame send on close socket).
    - add modbus exception EXP_NEGATIVE_ACKNOWLEDGE (code 0x07) to constants.
    - add last_error_txt() and last_except_txt() for produce human readable status.
    - add EXP_TXT, EXP_DETAILS and MB_ERR_TXT dicts to constants (text representation of codes).
    - update of the compatibility test for python version: remove 2.6/3.2/3.3, add 3.7/3.8/3.9.
    - conform to PEP 396 (add pyModbusTCP.__version__ field).

0.1.8 2018-10-15

    - fix ModbusServer: avoid hard coded TCP port (thanks to akobyl).
    - add stop() and is_run property to ModbusServer (thanks to Rugiewitz).

0.1.7 2018-08-20

    - fix ModbusServer issue on Windows (thanks to andreascian).

0.1.6 2018-05-14

    - fix multiple TCP packets recv issue in ModbusClient and ModbusServer (thanks Farin94).

